From: Chris Hibbert <hibbert@netcom.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Sep 94 15:50:10 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-149] FWD: copyright of the future

The basic idea here seems to be keeping hashes to identify copyrighted
works.  The extra content of this message is that someone (John
Garrett of IRC NRI) is implementing a repository for storing the
hashes so ownership can be tracked.  There's even enough info listed
so you could track the speaker down.  

I doubt if there have been test cases establishing that Email has the
same legal status as a "performance," but it's an interesting idea.
What implications would that have?  What is the copyright status of a
performance?

Chris

 ------- Forwarded Message

 Date: Thu, 15 Sep 1994 14:19:46 -0500
 Message-Id: <199409151919.AA16896@ux1.cso.uiuc.edu>
 To: cpsr-activists@cpsr.org
 From: marsha-w@uiuc.edu (Marsha-W)
 Subject: mindblowing copyright of the future

 One of the great things about being at the U of I is learning about
 the future before it hits...I went to a talk yesterday about how digital
 creations will be registered and copyrighted in the VERY near
 future.  John Garrett, the director of information resources corp. for
 national research initiatives (say that again?) has been walking a
 complicated procedure through.  I won't explain it in detail, but in
 the design, each of our future "works" will get a
 "handle"--sort of like a fingerprint--unique to each item.  We will
 register our works from our computers, and much of the particulars
 will be invisible to the user.   With these millions and zillions
 of handles, they'll be able to track creations.  I could get a handle
 for this note to you all, for instance...I think...

 The preliminary mechanism is in place.  If you want to secure a
 creation in the interim, (say a bit of computer code, anything digital,
 even an article, whatever), take the disk or print-out and seal
 it in a time-stamped envelope.  Soon that won't be necessary.

 I also learned that legally our e-mail to lists is the same
 as a "performance"  (and indeed, sometimes it is!!)--publishers can't
 claim you have already published something on the net, as I
 understand it, because of course it hasn't been published.. Just
 aired, like laundry, as it were....

 My head is still spinning...


